WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.785
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.785 --> 00:00:09.337
&gt;&gt; In the aftermath of the 9-11
I quit my career as a doctor so

00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:11.720
I could serve in the Air Force as a pilot.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.450
And at the end of my 13 year career,

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:20.220
I had the opportunity to work as
a dual qualified pilot and physician.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.750
I was an operational test pilot
in a nuclear bomber as well as

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:23.950
a flight surgeon.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.140
And there was 11 of us in
the entire Air Force at the time.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.244
And over my service, I had
the privilege and honor to work for so

00:00:32.244 --> 00:00:37.639
many incredibly inspiring,
talented, and motivating leaders.

00:00:37.639 --> 00:00:41.818
But over that time I also saw how often
our senior leaders fell short of what we

00:00:41.818 --> 00:00:45.880
needed them to be, and
that's why I'm on this stage today.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.680
I want to improve Air Force leadership and
make it the service that this nation

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.540
needs it to be, specifically,
by focusing on how we improve our leaders.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.960
To help you understand the leadership
gap that I'm talking about,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.590
I want to share with you
a tale of two leaders.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:06.220
One of my first commanders in one of
my earliest squadrons that I joined,

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.390
two months prior to me getting there,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.310
he had taken over that squadron after
the previous commander had been fired.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.867
There was a nuclear missile incident
that had reached national attention.

00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:22.465
And when I joined, this squadron was
under intense scrutiny by the entire

00:01:22.465 --> 00:01:27.520
Air Force and even higher up to make sure
that that mistake never happened again.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.850
And this meant working through
grueling inspections and

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:34.000
exercises, basically
simulating World War III.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.050
And if any of us made a mistake where we
decoded a nuclear message incorrectly,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:43.320
or had a procedural misstep,
entire base fails.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.150
Careers are ended.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:50.250
And it was during this intense
period of scrutiny that my wife and

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.370
I received some difficult news.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.480
We learned that Naomi was going
to need a kidney transplant.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.420
We didn't know when, but
it was going to happen.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.760
And that's when my commander,
who's call sign was Rude.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.810
He wasn't rude at all, just his call sign.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.640
But I remember he came up to me one day,
and

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.600
he said, Dave, listen,
I heard what's going on with your wife.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.940
I've talked to my boss, and we think it's
best that you just take some time off

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.220
I know we're severely undermanned for
this next inspection but

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.590
I think you need to focus on your family.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.455
The fact that he cared so
much about my family more than his career

00:02:28.455 --> 00:02:32.623
just made me want to work ten
times harder for this man.

00:02:35.596 --> 00:02:39.950
And that's simply because he
understood a very simple fact.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.680
There's a difference between what
the Air Force pays us to do and

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.886
what it asks us to do, and leadership
is what makes up that difference,

00:02:49.886 --> 00:02:52.430
leaders like Rude.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:58.540
Now over the next two years, we completed
all our inspections with flying colors.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.140
We completed a successful deployment.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:07.870
We also had a jet crash, and
we lost several of our own.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.180
But through all of this,
I was part of a team that was so

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:19.340
committed to each other,
their job, and their boss.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:20.816
And I wouldn't trade that for anything.

00:03:23.079 --> 00:03:27.570
Now contrast that experience with
another commander that I had.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.810
Went by the call sign of Panzer.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.140
And when he joined our squadron his
reputation already preceded him.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:39.760
And I remember early on he walked up to
one of the young pilots in our squadron

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.180
and he goes, hey captain?

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.440
I understand you're going to
weapons school right after this six

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.740
month deployment so you might be away from
your family for about an entire year.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.840
I was just thinking, what if we just
keep you back and you just go to

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.909
weapons school for six months
instead of going on this deployment.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.700
Captain looks at him up and goes well
gee sir that's not a bad idea at all.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.630
I mean, I just spent six
months deployed last year.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:10.310
My wife just delivered our
first child three weeks ago.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:14.210
Maybe it wouldn't be such a bad idea
if I just focused on preparing for

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:14.710
weapons school.

00:04:16.975 --> 00:04:17.940
Panzer looks at him again.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.515
That's not what I wanted to hear.

00:04:20.515 --> 00:04:26.590
When I ask you if you want to deploy,
the only thing I want to from you is sir,

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:28.760
yes sir, I'll do whatever you
want me to do for you sir!

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.650
Three days later, this pilot and

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.060
the rest of us are assembled in our
squadron getting ready to deploy.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:46.070
And he gets up in front
of the entire squadron.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.300
Understand we have all said
goodbye to our families.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.620
Children are still crying
in the parking lot, but

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:57.080
he gets up to address all of us and
he goes, today we're going to deploy.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.740
We're going to do what
we've been trained to do.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.610
Now we're all thinking we're going to get
one of those rousing patriotic speeches

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.310
you hear about in the movies.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.810
Maybe this guy isn't so bad after all.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.280
And you know what's so
great about deploying?

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.320
I get to get the hell away
from my wife and kids.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.230
I don't have to go to any of my
daughter's damn baseball games anymore.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.400
Look, I mean,
we're just as shocked as you are.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.722
I mean, this is the guys that's
going to lead us into combat?

00:05:26.231 --> 00:05:30.720
Turns out this incident was
really a sign of things to come.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.690
Because the next two years, we dealt
with a great deal of his malfeasance,

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.870
misconduct, and general misbehavior.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:46.230
And no amount of investigations and

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:51.070
evidence that piled up against what he
had done was going to make a difference.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:55.045
Basically, up the chain, the swept it
under the rug and made it go away.

00:05:58.047 --> 00:06:02.210
Those two years, really rather uneventful.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.170
But we,
as a squadron were left completely broken,

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:11.031
demoralized, doing the bare minimum
to get through each day and

00:06:11.031 --> 00:06:16.085
counting the days that we're going to
be able to get out of this unit.

00:06:19.874 --> 00:06:23.318
I wish there was a better
ending to this story, but

00:06:23.318 --> 00:06:27.256
the fact of the matter is,
Rude, my earlier commander,

00:06:27.256 --> 00:06:31.210
his career plateaued,
he's no longer in the Air Force.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:36.590
Panzer is in line to be a general
in the US Air Force today.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.770
Why does this matter to anyone here?

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.970
Well, the US Air Force is in
a serious crisis right now,

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.130
that affects our national security.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:51.670
Pilots are getting out of the Air Force
as soon as their committment's up.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:54.540
There are cockpits going
unfilled right now and

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.960
it poses a serious threat to our security.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.520
We're not able to do our mission anymore.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:02.580
Most people aren't aware of this fact.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:08.780
And what I've watched is
the Air Force talk about how

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:10.380
we need to pay the pilots more.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.820
Change a deployment schedule.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.649
And that's missing the heart
of the matter here.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:19.630
Pilots aren't quitting their jobs.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.704
They're quitting their bosses,

00:07:21.704 --> 00:07:26.067
bosses who don't care about their
people as much as they care about their

00:07:26.067 --> 00:07:30.800
own promotion, bosses who won't speak
truth to power, bosses like Panzer.

00:07:33.791 --> 00:07:38.712
I've had the great opportunity to spend
the last year at business school at

00:07:38.712 --> 00:07:40.240
Stanford.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:43.680
And I've looked over how
we can fix this problem.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.090
I've looked at the private sector,
looked at the HR practices and

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.440
the talent management strategies
that I think will make a difference.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:57.170
And three of the top things that I think
the Air Force can do to really make it

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:02.250
into what this nation needs it to be are,
hiring a professional search firm,

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:05.820
360 evaluations, and
neuropsychiatric evaluations.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.280
And let me explain what I'm talking about.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.790
Hire a professional search
firm that can come in and

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:17.060
identify exactly what kind of leader
you need, what kind of skills,

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:20.390
characteristics and
experiences these leaders should have.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:24.800
And once you've built your target you
can just work backwards from there and

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.480
develop a set of promotion criteria
that can identify, develop, and

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.719
select those specific leaders.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:38.762
Right now, in my opinion,
we have an arbitrary gauntlet of promotion

00:08:38.762 --> 00:08:43.687
criteria designed to see who wants to
be promoted the most instead of having

00:08:43.687 --> 00:08:48.633
promotion criteria developed to see
what kind of leaders we need the most.

00:08:50.833 --> 00:08:55.640
Number two, 360 evaluations,
we've all done this in business school.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:00.160
And a lot of companies,
this is standard operating procedure.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.370
I would argue that
the people at the bottom

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:08.380
of the chain are often in the best
position to identify the best leaders.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:12.220
Look, I think we can all agree.

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:16.290
Anyone can look good for the priest on
Sunday morning, it's how you act on

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.920
Saturday night that tells me really
what kind of person you are.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.960
And third, neuropsychiatric testing,
now this one really caught me off guard.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.930
I didn't realize that so many companies
were already doing this to make sure that

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.910
they identified and
promoted the best leader.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:39.210
Turns out Army Special Forces and
Navy SEALs already employ neropsychiatric

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:42.940
testing to pick through
all of their recruits.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:47.810
You can be the biggest, fastest,
strongest recruit in Navy SEAL training.

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:51.090
But if your test comes back
showing narcissistic or

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:56.000
sociopathic tendencies,
you're done, you're out.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:02.175
Special Forces don't tolerate people they
can't trust, neither should the Air Force.

00:10:02.175 --> 00:10:06.409
Conveniently, the Air Force has all
this data for every one of its pilots.

00:10:06.409 --> 00:10:09.573
I and everyone else spent five
hours taking this IQ test and

00:10:09.573 --> 00:10:13.330
personality test before we
can go to pilot training.

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:17.408
It's just that they only revisit those
results in the event that one of us has

00:10:17.408 --> 00:10:20.737
a head injury and we need to be
cleared before we can fly again.

00:10:22.991 --> 00:10:28.680
So professional search firms, 360
evaluations, and neuropsychiatric testing.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.230
That's what I think would
make a real difference, and

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:34.740
this is pretty standard
in the private sector.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:38.300
So why aren't we moving the needle
on this in the military if things

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:39.090
are getting so bad?

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:45.110
Well I've talked to military leaders,
political

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:50.510
scientists, and famous authors on
this topic and they all agree.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:55.220
This is a growing problem and
something needs to be done about this.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:59.080
But the only thing that's going to fix it
is an attack on the scale of Pearl Harbor.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.100
What, why do we need
to sacrifice blood and

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:08.550
treasure to get an organization
to embrace true leadership?

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:15.155
I'm not asking for $1 billion and I'm not
asking to send a Tesla Roadster to Mars.

00:11:15.155 --> 00:11:19.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's simple

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.420
standard private sector HR practices.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.460
But I will admit, it is difficult.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:31.510
The three things that any organization
is going to need to embrace to implement

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:38.750
these decisions and
these actions are first, have the strength

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:43.300
to look in the mirror and embrace the
uncomfortable truth staring back at you.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:49.310
Two, discipline, have the discipline

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.556
to take action before you've
run out of all other options.

00:11:54.556 --> 00:11:59.380
And finally moral clarity,

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:03.406
have the moral clarity to
put purpose above promotion.

00:12:03.406 --> 00:12:05.999
Thank you.

00:12:05.999 --> 00:12:10.796
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:12:10.796 --> 00:12:15.919
[MUSIC]

